INTRODUCTION TO IA-32 INSTRUCTION ENCODING
===========================================

1. INTRODUCTION TO IA-32 INSTRUCTION ENCODING
----------------------------------------------
IA-32 (x86-32) machine instructions are variable-length, built from an
ordered sequence of optional and mandatory byte fields. Getting comfortable
with how each field is encoded is really the foundation for anything
lower-level -- writing an assembler, a disassembler, or even just a
debugger that needs to make sense of raw instruction bytes.

An instruction is put together from up to five pieces, always in the same
order: the opcode, the ModR/M byte, the SIB byte, a displacement, and an
immediate value. Not every instruction uses every field -- the opcode
itself decides which of the remaining fields actually show up.


2. INSTRUCTION BYTE DIAGRAM
----------------------------
OPCODE | MOD R/M | SIB | DISPLACEMENT | IMMEDIATE

- OPCODE -- the primary opcode, 1 to 3 bytes long.
- MOD R/M -- 1 byte, present when the instruction touches a register or
  memory operand.
- SIB (Scale-Index-Base) -- 1 byte, only shows up for certain memory
  addressing forms.
- Displacement -- 1, 2, or 4 bytes, present when the addressing form needs
  a constant offset.
- Immediate -- 1, 2, or 4 bytes, present when the instruction carries a
  literal operand value.


3. OPCODE STRUCTURE
--------------------
The ModR/M byte breaks down into three bit fields that together pick the
addressing mode and register:

  MOD          | REG/OPCODE   | R/M
  2 bits       | 3 bits       | 3 bits

The SIB byte follows the same pattern, used for scaled-index memory
addressing:

  Scale        | Index        | Base
  2 bits       | 3 bits       | 3 bits

The Scale field just picks a multiplier for the index register:

  Scale | Multiplier
  00    | x1
  01    | x2
  10    | x4
  11    | x8


