6. OPCODE FORMAT NOTATION (/r, /0, /5, +rd)
---------------------------------------------
- /r -- the ModR/M byte's REG field holds an actual register operand
  (source or destination), covering register-to-register and
  register-to-memory forms.

- /digit (e.g. /0, /5, /6) -- here REG isn't a register at all, it's a
  fixed 3-bit opcode extension. The digit listed is the exact bit pattern
  REG must carry for that instruction -- it's how one opcode byte (like
  81 or FF) can stand in for several different instructions, distinguished
  only by those REG bits.

- +rd -- the register is folded directly into the low 3 bits of the
  opcode byte itself, skipping ModR/M entirely. Add the register's 3-bit
  code (0-7) to the base opcode value to get the final byte. Used by the
  short forms PUSH r32 (50+rd) and POP r32 (58+rd).

- ib / id -- a suffix flagging that an immediate follows: ib means a
  1-byte immediate, id means a 4-byte (doubleword) immediate.


